  "slug": "graphene-engineering-5eb6",
  "summary": "New preprints link displacement-field control, layer number and twist to anomalous Hall metals, hysteresis and valley-imbalanced superconductivity in graphene.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is control of band topology and valley/pseudospin order in graphene multilayers via displacement field, stacking and twist. In ABA trilayer graphene, a displacement field inverts band edges, reverses Berry curvature and produces valley splitting. Experiments on Bernal pentalayer graphene report displacement-field-tunable first-order transitions with hysteretic Hall responses. Hartree-Fock work on Bernal tetralayer graphene predicts interaction-driven ordering of multiple pseudospins and anomalous Hall metals. In 17-layer rhombohedral graphene, superconductivity with valley-imbalanced Fermi pockets is enhanced by in-plane field. In twisted monolayer-N-layer rhombohedral graphene, narrow topological bands arise from Dirac-cone/surface-state hybridization tuned by layer number. Magic-angle twisted bilayer work finds hidden f-wave TIVC order near nu=-2, and Floquet driving is predicted to induce valley polarization and Chern transitions. Parallel lines treat twisted TMDs (an angle-independent microscopic continuum model; a non-quantized Chern metal in a holon model) and 3D quantum anomalous Hall systems from coupled Weyl semimetals. All are preprints.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
